{"id":"95ff0cbe-fd0f-4b93-a635-bdbd3fa6eed6","object":"chat.completion","created":1784996280,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError('No nodes provided')\\n\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children_map[nid] = []\\n\\n    # Identify root and build children\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError('Multiple roots')\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            parent = n['parent']\\n            if parent not in children_map:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {parent} not found')\\n            children_map[parent].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n\\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol \\\"{sym}\\\" not present in any node')\\n\\n    # Initial counts for required symbols (remaining)\\n    remaining_counts = {sym: symbol_counts[sym] for sym in required_symbols}\\n\\n    # Build list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        # Check children retained\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed for cid in children_map[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols still present after removal\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            if remaining_counts[sym] <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        # Remove this node\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            remaining_counts[sym] -= 1\\n\\n    retained = sorted(nid for nid in node_map if nid not in removed)\\n\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removal_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\\n    }\\n\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm based on the provided description. The case payload contains a tree of nodes (with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The task: remove removable nodes in a specific order, while preserving required symbols and ensuring the tree remains \"grammar-valid\"? Actually the description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but the provided payload only has nodes with parent pointers forming a tree. There's no explicit grammar. Perhaps the grammar is implicitly defined by the symbols? The task seems to be: given a tree, we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible, but not removing nodes whose symbol is required (required_symbols). Also, we cannot remove a node if it has children that are retained (i.e., we can only remove leaf nodes?). The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means we can only remove a node if it is removable, its symbol not in required_symbols, and it has no children that are being retained. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removal, the tree must still contain at least one node for each required symbol. And we process deeper nodes first (depth descending), tie-break by node ID (string? stable). Return removed_node_ids (in removal order) and retained_node_ids (the IDs of all nodes that are not removed). The output must be sorted? The schema says removed_node_ids is an array of strings (order presumably removal order), retained_node_ids is an array of strings (probably sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted, removed_node_ids in order of removal. Need to implement.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a mapping from node id to node dict.\n- Determine children for each node.\n- Identify root (parent null).\n- Mark nodes that are required_symbols (if any node has symbol in required_symbols, that node is \"required\"? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols; any node with that symbol is required? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So nodes with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed. So we treat them as non-removable.\n- Also root is non-removable.\n- Process nodes in order: by depth descending, then by node ID string (tie break). Check if removable and not required symbol, and not root, and has no children that are not yet removed (i.e., all children have been removed or are scheduled to be removed? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if a child is being retained (not removed), then cannot remove this node. So we can only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose children are all removed). Since we process deeper first, children are deeper, so when processing a node, its children might already be considered for removal; if they are removable and satisfy conditions, they would have been removed earlier? But careful: children might be retained if they are required or not removable. So we must check at the moment: if any child is still present (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node.\n- Also after removal, required symbols must still be represented somewhere in the remaining tree. So even if node is not required symbol itself, if removing it would cause a required symbol to disappear (i.e., because that symbol only appears in the subtree being removed), we must not remove. But we can check: For each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol must remain after removal. So before tentatively removing a node, check if there exists another node (not in the subtree being removed) that has that required symbol. Since we only remove one node at a time, we can check if for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol that is not the node being removed and not in its descendants (if we remove a node, its descendants are also removed? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It might imply removing the node and all its descendants? The typical Perses algorithm removes a subtree and replaces it with nothing? But the description: \"Remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Usually in grammar-guided reduction, you can delete a node and its children (since they are children). Yes, if you remove a node, you remove its entire subtree. The description says \"Never remove a node with retained children\" — that implies you can't remove a node if any of its children are retained, but if you remove the node, all children are removed. So it's about legality of removal: you can only remove a leaf (or node whose children are all already removed) because children are deeper and would have been processed first. But if they are required, they cannot be removed, so the parent cannot be removed. So effectively we can only remove leaf nodes that are removable and not required. However, the description \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" indicates that when we consider removing a node, if any child is still present (retained), we cannot remove because that would invalidate the tree? Actually it might be that removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree; but then having retained children is contradictory. So the condition ensures that we only remove nodes that have no children (or whose children are all scheduled for removal? But children are deeper, they would be considered earlier, so if a child is removable and not required, it would be removed first, making the parent a leaf). So algorithm: iterate nodes in order (depth descending). For each node, if it is removable, not root, not required symbol, and currently it has no children (i.e., all children have been removed already), then we can remove it, provided that after removal (i.e., removing this node from the tree) all required symbols are still present somewhere in the remaining tree. But note: removing this node might remove the only occurrence of some required symbol, even though the node itself doesn't have required symbol? Actually if the node does not have required symbol, its subtree might contain required symbols. If those required symbols are only in this subtree, then removing would lose them. So we need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol outside the subtree of this node (including the node itself if it had the symbol). So we can precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Then when considering removal, check that for each required symbol, the total count of that symbol in the whole tree minus the count in the subtree of the node (if we remove the node) is at least 1. Since we remove nodes one by one, we could update counts, but simpler: precompute subtree symbol counts. For each node, compute a multiset (or set) of symbols in its subtree. Also compute total symbol counts. Then when considering removal, check that for each required symbol, total_count[symbol] - subtree_count[node][symbol] >= 1. If true