st, by the time we consider a node, its children are already removed (if they were removable). So its descendants may already be removed. So the set of nodes that will be removed is just this node itself, because all descendants are already removed. But what if some child is not removable? Then the node cannot be removed because it has retained children. So the only case where we consider removal is when all children have been removed. So the subtree is already empty except for this node. So the only node being removed is the node itself. That simplifies: we only remove leaf nodes. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" reduces to: if the node itself has a required symbol, then removing it would lose that symbol (unless there is another node with that symbol elsewhere). So we just need to check that for each required symbol, either the node does not have that symbol, or there is at least one other node (outside this node) that has that symbol. Since descendants are already removed, we don't need to worry about them. So we can precompute total counts of each required symbol across all nodes. Then when considering a node, if its symbol is in required_symbols, we check if total count of that symbol > 1; if yes, removal okay; if not, cannot remove. That matches the fixture examples? Let's check fixture payload: In first case, required symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. Nodes: root (symbol root), n1 (symbol s1), n2 (symbol s2, removable true), n3 (s0), n4 (s1, removable true), n5 (s2), n6 (s0, removable true). Depths: root(0), n1(1), n2(1), n3(1), n4(2), n5(2), n6(2). Required symbols: root and s1. So nodes with root: only root. Nodes with s1: n1 and n4. So root cannot be removed (non-removable). n1 has s1, but there are two s1 nodes, so if n1 were removable, we could remove? But n1 is not removable (removable: false). n4 is removable and has s1, but there is another s1 (n1), so we could remove n4? But we need to check children: n4's parent is n2, depth 2. n4 has no children? The payload shows nodes: n4 parent n2, no children listed. So it's a leaf. So n4 is removable, symbol s1, required, but another s1 exists (n1), so removal okay. However, n2 (parent of n4) has symbol s2 (not required), removable true. After removing n4, n2 becomes a leaf? But n2 has child n4, which if removed, n2 no longer has children. So n2 could be considered later. n6 is removable, symbol s0 (not required), no children. n3 has symbol s0, n5 symbol s2. The algorithm would process deeper first: nodes at depth 2: n4, n5, n6. Order tie-break by ID: n4, n5, n6 (lexicographic? n4 < n5 < n6). For each: n4: removable true, symbol s1 required, but total s1 count = 2 > 1, so can remove. n5: not removable (false). n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, can remove. Then depth 1: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable, symbol s2 not required), n3 (not removable). Process order: n1, n2, n3 (tie-breaking by ID? n1 < n2 < n3). But after removal of n4 and n6, n2 has child n4 removed, so no retained children? n2 still has n4 removed, so no children. So n2 can be removed if it doesn't break required symbols. n2 symbol s2 not required. Removal of n2 would remove itself only. Required symbols: root and s1 are still present (root and n1). So n2 can be removed. Then n1 and n3 cannot be removed (non-removable). So removed order: n4, n6, n2. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible. The output would be removed_node_ids: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"] (order of removal), retained_node_ids sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]. Let's check if that matches expected? Not provided but seems consistent.\n\nNow, there is also mention of \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"replacements\" but not used. The fixture doesn't have replacement info. Probably for this task we only do deletion (removal) not replacement. So we implement as above.\n\nEdge cases: If there are multiple nodes with same symbol, removal is okay if count > 1. If a required symbol appears only in a non-removable node, that node stays. If a required symbol appears only in a removable node, that node cannot be removed unless there is another occurrence (which there isn't), so it will be retained. So algorithm: while there is any node that can be removed, repeatedly process in order. Since we process deeper first and remove leaf nodes, we can do a single pass in order of decreasing depth and then increasing ID. Because after removing a node, its parent becomes a candidate later in the same pass (since parent has shallower depth and will be processed later). So we can do one pass and for each node if conditions hold, remove it. But need to ensure that we don't skip nodes that become candidates after previous removals in the same depth? Since we process deeper first, after removing a leaf, its parent is at shallower depth and will be processed later. So one pass suffices. However, there might be a scenario where removing a node causes another node at same depth to become eligible (e.g., two sibling leaves, one is removed, the parent becomes leaf, but parent is deeper? Actually parent is shallower, so not same depth). So one pass is fine.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Build node dict mapping id to node.\n2. Build children list for each node.\n3. Find root.\n4. Compute total counts of each required symbol (symbol -> count).\n5. Sort nodes by (-depth, id) where id is string natural sort (ASCII order likely fine).\n6. Maintain set of removed IDs.\n7. For each node in sorted order:\n   - Skip if already removed.\n   - If node['removable'] is False, skip.\n   - If node['id'] == root, skip (root id is \"root\").\n   - If node['symbol'] in required_symbols: if total_count[node['symbol']] - (1 if node['symbol'] in required_symbols?) Actually we need to check after removals. We'll maintain a dynamic count of remaining occurrences. But easier: after removal, we can just check if there is at least one other node with that symbol not removed. Since we process one node at a time, we can check if there exists some other node with same symbol that is not removed and not this node. We can precompute a mapping symbol->list of node IDs. Then for each candidate, we check if there is at least one node in that list that is not removed and not the candidate. But that's O(N) each. Better: maintain a count of remaining nodes for each required symbol. Initially compute total counts. When we remove a node, decrement count for its symbol. Then condition: if node's symbol is required, its count must be >1 before removal (i.e., count > 1). Because after removal it becomes 1 (or 0 if only one). So we check if current count > 1.\n   - Check children: for each child in children list of node, if that child's ID is not removed, then skip (cannot remove because retained children). Since children are deeper, they have been processed earlier; if they were removable and conditions met, they would have been removed. So if any child remains, skip.\n   - If passes, add node id to removed set, decrement its symbol count (if symbol is required), and record removal order.\n8. After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids = sorted list of all node ids not in removed set.\n9. Return { \"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained }.\n\nNeed to handle case where input has no nodes? Probably not.\n\nAlso need to ensure correct tie-break: depth descending, then node ID ascending (string comparison). Python's default sort for strings is lexicographic ASCII, which is fine for IDs like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. But note IDs like \"root\" will be at depth 0, after depth -1? Depth descending means highest depth first. So we sort by -depth, then id.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). case is the payload dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Return dict with keys as per schema.\n\nEdge Cases: If required_symbols list is empty, then we can remove any removable leaf that is not root and has no children. That's fine.\n\nIf there is no root? Assume there is exactly one root (parent null). If multiple, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso if required symbol not present in any node? Should we raise ValueError? The description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" If required_symbols contains a symbol not found in any node, then it's impossible to satisfy, so we should raise ValueError. But the fixture shows root and s1 are present. We'll check that all required symbols have at least one node. Also check that there is a root.\n\nAlso check that every node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Not strictly needed but to be safe.\n\nWe'll implement with standard library only.\n\nNow produce the final answer: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source code string (escaped). Must not use markdown fences.\n\nWrite code accordingly."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4709,"total_tokens":6878,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4055},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}